<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Atahualpa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1502–1533</span> · Also known as Atawallpa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Atahualpa was the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sapa-inca">Sapa Inca</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca Empire</a> before the Spanish conquest. A son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huayna-capac">Huayna Capac</a>, he defeated his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huascar">Huáscar</a> in the civil war of 1529–1532, but in November 1532 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a> captured him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cajamarca">Cajamarca</a>. Although he paid a huge ransom in gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, the Spanish executed him in July 1533.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/atahualpa.jpg" alt="Atahualpa" width="320" height="413" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Atahuallpa,_Inca_XIIII_From_Berlin_Ethnologisches_Museum,_Staatliche_Museen,_Berlin,_Germany.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
